<section xml:id="sec-ch05-02-FundamentalTheoremOfLineIntegrals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals</title>

  <note>
    <title>Conservative Vector Fields</title>

    <p>
      As we discussed, given a vector field <m>\vec{F}</m>, two points
      <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> in its domain ( <m>P</m> could be equal to
      <m>Q</m> ), and two different curves <m>C_{1}</m> and <m>C_{2}</m>
      connecting <m>P</m> to <m>Q</m>, in most cases,
      <md>
        \int_{C_{1}} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s \neq \int_{C_{2}} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            In general, different curves connecting two points give
            different values for line integrals.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      However, there is a way we can guarantee that for any path <m>C</m>
      with starting point <m>P</m> and ending point <m>Q</m>, then
      <m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s</m> always has the same value
      independent of which path you take. We do this by working with
      conservative vector fields.
    </p>
  </note>

  <definition>
    <title>Conservative Vector Fields</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A vector field
        <m>\vec{F}=\langle f(x, y, z), g(x, y, z), h(x, y, z)\rangle</m> is
        said to be conservative if there exists a scalar valued function
        <m>\varphi(x, y, z)</m>, with continuous partial derivatives, such
        that
        <md>
          \vec{F}=\nabla \varphi=\left\langle\varphi_{x}, \varphi_{y}, \varphi_{z}\right\rangle
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Equivalently, if
        <md>
          <mrow>\varphi_{x} \amp =f(x, y, z)</mrow>
          <mrow>\varphi_{y} \amp =g(x, y, z)</mrow>
          <mrow>\varphi_{z} \amp =h(x, y, z)</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>

      <p>
        We call such a function <m>\varphi</m> a potential function for
        <m>\vec{F}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-02-ex-24-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify that <m>\varphi(x, y)=x^3+2y+y^2</m> is a potential function
        for <m>\vec{F}=\langle 3x^2, 2y+2\rangle</m> (so that <m>\vec{F}</m>
        is conservative).
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md>
          \left. \begin{matrix} \varphi_x = 3 x^2\\ \varphi_y  = 2+2y \end{matrix} \right\} \Rightarrow \nabla \varphi (x,y) =  \langle 3x^2 , 2+2y \rangle = \vec F
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      In general, finding a function <m>\varphi</m> such that
      <m>\nabla \varphi=\vec{F}</m> is hard, hence, we developed an easier
      test to determine whether a vector field is conservative.
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>From now on, we will assume the regions we discuss are open
      (don't contain their boundary).</alert>
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Test for Conservative Vector Fields</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>\vec{F}=\langle f, g, h\rangle</m> be a vector field defined on
      a connected and simply connected region <m>D</m>, and suppose that
      <m>f, g</m>, and <m>h</m> have continuous partial derivatives on
      <m>D</m>. Then <m>\vec{F}</m> is conservative on <m>D</m> if and only
      if
      <md>
        \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}=\frac{\partial g}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}=\frac{\partial h}{\partial x}, \quad \text { and } \quad \frac{\partial g}{\partial z}=\frac{\partial h}{\partial y}
      </md>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If <m>\vec{F}=\langle f, g\rangle</m>, then you only have to check the
      condition
      <md>
        \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}=\frac{\partial g}{\partial x}
      </md>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <p>
    This is an example of how a connected and simply connected region
    looks/does not look like:
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-02-connected-regions">
    <caption>From openstax vol 3</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_11_14_290fb7938ef82f914ca4g-02-1.png" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>On the first row there are three simply connected regions: the region enclosed by a trapezoid, the region enclosed by a simple closed smooth curve and the region enclosed by a hexagon. On the second row there are three connected but not simply connected regions: the region enclosed by a trapezoid but excluding the region of a disk inside, the region enclosed by a simple closed smooth curve but excluding the region of a disk inside, and the region enclosed by a hexagon but excluding the region of a disk as well as another circular region from its interior. On the third row there is a region that is not connected, consisting of the regions enclosed by two non-intersecting simple closed smooth curves.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>On the first row there are three simply connected regions: the region enclosed by a trapezoid, the region enclosed by a simple closed smooth curve and the region enclosed by a hexagon. On the second row there are three connected but not simply connected regions: the region enclosed by a trapezoid but excluding the region of a disk inside, the region enclosed by a simple closed smooth curve but excluding the region of a disk inside, and the region enclosed by a hexagon but excluding the region of a disk as well as another circular region from its interior. On the third row there is a region that is not connected, consisting of the regions enclosed by two non-intersecting simple closed smooth curves.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-02-ex-24-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether the following vector fields are conservative over
        their domain:
      </p>

      <p>
        All the components of the vector fields below are polynomials
        defined on the entire <m>xy</m>-plane. Hence we can apply the
        aforementioned test.
      </p>

      <p>
        (a) <m>\vec{F}=\left\langle 3x^2+2xy+y^2, x^2+2xy+1 \right\rangle</m>
      </p>

      <p>(b) <m>\vec{F}=\langle yz+y, xz+x,xy+x+y\rangle</m>.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>f(x,y)= 3x^2+2xy+y^2 , \ g(x,y) = x^2+2xy+1</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y  = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(3x^2+2xy+y^2) = 2x +2y, \ g_x =\frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2+2xy+1)=2x+2y</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F \text{ is conservative since they are equal}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>f(x,y,z)=  yz+y , \ g(x,y,z) = xz+x, \ h(x,y,z) =  xy+x+y</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y  = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(yz+y) =z +1, \ g_x = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(xz+x)=z+1</mrow>
          <mrow>\ f_z =\frac{\partial}{\partial z}(yz+y) = y, \ \ h_x = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(xy+x+y) = y+1 \text{ they are not equal}</mrow>
          <mrow>g_z = \frac{\partial}{\partial z}(xz+x) = x, \ \ h_y = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(xy+x+y) = x+1 \text{  they are not equal}</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F \text{ is not conservative.}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      Once we know a vector field is conservative, now we can put in the
      effort to find a potential function for it.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-02-ex-24-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find a potential function for the conservative vector field
        <m>\vec{F}=\left\langle 3x^2+2xy+y^2, x^2+2xy+1 \right\rangle</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        A potential function <m>\varphi</m> would satisfy
        <m>\nabla \varphi = \vec F</m> and that would imply that
        <m>\varphi_x = 3x^2+2xy+y^2</m> and <m>\varphi_y = x^2+2xy+1</m>.
        Now:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int \varphi_x \ dx = \int 3x^2+2xy+y^2 \ dx = x^3 +x^2y + y^2x +f(y)</mrow>
          <mrow>\int \varphi_y \ dy = \int x^2 +2xy+1 dy = x^2y+xy^2+y +g(x)</mrow>
        </md>
        Notice that the terms of the above polynomials that depend on both
        <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are equal, as one would expect since their
        corresponding partials would appear in both <m>\varphi_x</m> and
        <m>\varphi_y</m>. This allows for <m>f(y)=y</m> (the non-common
        part of the polynomials that depends only on <m>y</m>) and
        <m>g(x) = x^3</m> (the non-common part of the polynomials that
        depends only on <m>x</m>). Hence
        <m>\varphi (x,y) = x^2y+xy^2+x^3+y</m>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-02-ex-24-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find a potential function for the conservative vector field
        <m>\vec{F}= \left\langle 2 x y-9 x^{2}, 2 y+x^{2}+1\right\rangle</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        A potential function <m>\varphi</m> would satisfy
        <m>\nabla \varphi = \vec F</m> and that would imply that
        <m>\varphi_x = 2 x y-9 x^{2}</m> and <m>\varphi_y = 2y+x^{2}+1</m>.
        Now:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int \varphi_x \ dx = \int 2 x y-9 x^2 \ dx = x^2y -3x^3 +f(y)</mrow>
          <mrow>\int \varphi_y \ dy = \int 2y+x^{2}+1 dy = y^2+x^2y+y  +g(x)</mrow>
        </md>
        Notice that the terms of the above polynomials that depend on both
        <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are equal, as one would expect since their
        corresponding partials would appear in both <m>\varphi_x</m> and
        <m>\varphi_y</m>. This allows for <m>f(y)=y^2+y</m> (the non-common
        part of the polynomials that depends only on <m>y</m>) and
        <m>g(x) = -3x^3</m> (the non-common part of the polynomials that
        depends only on <m>x</m>). Hence
        <m>\varphi (x,y) = x^2y-3x^3+y^2+y</m>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>Conservative Vector Fields and Path Independence</title>

    <p>
      A vector field <m>\vec{F}</m>, defined over an open connected region,
      is path independent if and only if it is conservative.
    </p>
  </remark>

