  </p>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-02-ex-24-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the vector field
        <m>\vec{F}=\left\langle xy^{2}, y x^2+1\right\rangle</m> and the two
        paths shown above connecting the points <m>(0,0)</m> and
        <m>(1,1)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>(a) Determine whether <m>\vec{F}</m> is conservative.</p>

      <p>(b) Evaluate the vector line integral over the first path.</p>

      <p>
        (c) Use your answer for part (b) and path independence to evaluate
        the line integral over the second path
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        All the components of the vector field are polynomials defined on
        the entire <m>xy</m>-plane. Hence one can apply the aforementioned
        test.
        <md>
          f_y =\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(xy^2) = 2xy, \ g_x=\frac{\partial}{\partial x} (yx^2+1) = 2xy, \text{ and since those are equal, }\vec F\text{ is conservative}
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec r(t)=\langle 0,0 \rangle + t \langle 1,1 \rangle = \langle t,t \rangle, \ 0\leq t \leq 1, \ \vec r'(t) = \langle 1,1 \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F (\vec r (t)) = \langle t^3 ,  t^3+1 \rangle, \  \vec F (\vec r (t))\cdot \vec r ' (t) = t^3+t^3+1 = 2t^3+1</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^1 \vec F (\vec r (t))\cdot \vec r ' (t) \ dt = \int_0^1 2t^3+1 \ dt =\left . \frac{t^4}{2}+t \right |_0^1 = \frac{1}{2}+1  = \frac{3}{2}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        From the fact that <m>\vec F</m> is conservative we have path
        independence and hence the answer is the same as part (b)
        <m>\frac{3}{2}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!--\setcounter{tcb@cnt@example}{4}
  \begin{example}[Continuation of 24.5]
  \vspace{0.5cm}\end{example}-->

  <theorem>
    <title>Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>\vec{F}</m> is conservative over an open, connected,
        simply connected region <m>D</m> and that the function
        <m>\varphi</m> satisfies <m>\nabla \varphi=\vec{F}</m>. Then
        <md>
          \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s=\varphi(B)-\varphi(A)
        </md>,
        where <m>C</m> is any (piece-wise) smooth path from the point
        <m>A</m> to the point <m>B</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-02-ex-24-6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify that the function <m>\phi (x,y)=\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+y</m>
        satisfies <m>\nabla \phi=\vec{F}</m>, where
        <m>\vec{F}=\left\langle xy^{2}, y x^2+1\right\rangle</m>. Use the
        fundamental theorem of line integrals to evaluate the vector line
        integral over the path shown below.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-02-ex-24-6">
        <caption>On the background a vector field, on the foreground the straight line connecting the points (0,0) and (1,1)</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_14_290fb7938ef82f914ca4g-06. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-02-ex-24-6" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_11_14_290fb7938ef82f914ca4g-06.png">
              <shortdescription>On the background a vector field, on the foreground the straight line connecting the points (0,0) and (1,1)</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>On the background a vector field, on the foreground the straight line connecting the points <m>(0,0)</m> and <m>(1,1)</m></p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <md>
          y= x, 0 \leq x \leq 1
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\varphi_x = xy^2, \ \varphi_y = x^2y+1 \Rightarrow \nabla \varphi = \vec F</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_C \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds = \varphi (1,1)-\varphi (0,0) = \frac{1}{2}+1 -\frac{0}{2}-0 = \frac{3}{2}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      The Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals tells us what happens when
      we integrate a conservative vector field over a simple and closed
      curve.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Loop Property</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>R</m> be an open and connected region. Then <m>\vec{F}</m> is
      conservative on <m>R</m> if and only if
      <m>\oint_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s=0</m> for all simple, closed,
      and piecewise smooth curves in <m>R</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-02-ex-24-7">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Explain why the loop property holds if you start with a
        conservative vector field <m>\vec{F}</m> and a SCPS curve <m>C</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Assume that a SCPS curve <m>C</m> has <m>n</m> smooth pieces,
        <m>C_1</m> from point <m>P_1</m> to point <m>P_2</m>, <m>C_2</m>
        from <m>P_2</m> to <m>P_3</m>, <m>\ldots</m>, and <m>C_n</m> from
        <m>P_{n}</m> all the way back to <m>P_1</m> and assume that
        <m>\nabla \varphi = \vec F</m> . Then
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int_C \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds = \int_{C_1} \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds + \int_{C_2} \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds +\cdots + \int_{C_n} \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds =</mrow>
          <mrow>\left ( \varphi (P_2) - \varphi (P_1)\right ) +\left ( \varphi (P_3) - \varphi (P_2)\right ) + \cdots + \left ( \varphi (P_1) - \varphi (P_n)\right ) =</mrow>
          <mrow>\text{by reordering one can see that}</mrow>
          <mrow>- \varphi (P_1) + \cancelto{0}{\varphi (P_2)  - \varphi (P_2)} +   \cancelto{0}{\varphi (P_3)  - \varphi (P_3)} + \cdots +   \cancelto{0}{\varphi (P_n )  - \varphi (P_n)} +  \varphi (P_1) =</mrow>
          <mrow>- \varphi (P_1)+\varphi (P_1) = 0</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      We use the integral symbol <m>\oint</m> to emphasize we are
      integrating over a simple and closed curve.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-02-ex-24-8">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate <m>\oint_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s</m> where
        <m>\vec{F}=\langle y, x\rangle</m> and <m>C</m> is the unit circle
        (with a given orientation).
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The unit circle is a SCPS curve. Notice that <m>\vec F</m> is
        conservative since <m>\varphi (x,y) = xy</m> is a potential function
        for it. One can also notice that all the components of the vector
        field are polynomials defined on the entire <m>xy</m>-plane. Hence
        one can apply the test
        <m>f_y = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}y = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}x = g_x</m>
        which yields the same conclusion. By the fundamental theorem of
        line integrals and the loop property
        <md>
          \oint_C \vec F\cdot \vec T ds = 0
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      The test given for conservative vector fields only works over
      connected and simply connected regions.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-02-ex-24-9">
    <statement>
      <p>
        You can verify that
        <m>\vec{F}=\left\langle\frac{-y}{x^{2}+y^{2}}, \frac{x}{x^{2}+y^{2}}\right\rangle</m>
        satisfies
        <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}=\frac{\partial g}{\partial x}</m>,
        however, the vector line integral of <m>\vec{F}</m> over
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle\cos (t), \sin (t)\rangle, 0 \leq t \leq 2 \pi</m>,
        is <m>2 \pi</m> instead of zero. Why is this the case?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Indeed
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>f_y =\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2} = \frac{-(x^2+y^2) - (-y)(2y)}{(x^2+y^2)^2} = \frac{-(x^2+y^2)+2y^2}{(x^2+y^2)^2} = \frac{y^2-x^2}{(x^2+y^2)^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>g_x = \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \frac{x}{x^2+y^2} = \frac{(x^2+y^2) - x(2x)}{(x^2+y^2)^2} = \frac{(x^2+y^2)-2x^2}{(x^2+y^2)^2} = \frac{y^2-x^2}{(x^2+y^2)^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec r'(t) = \langle -\sin(t) , \cos (t) \rangle, \ \vec F (\vec r (t)) = \langle -\sin(t) , \cos (t) \rangle , \    \vec F (\vec r (t))\cdot \vec r' (t) = \sin^2 (t) + \cos^2 (t) =1</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^{2\pi} \vec F (\vec r (t))\cdot \vec r' (t)\ dt  = \int_0^{2\pi} 1 \ dt =2\pi</mrow>
        </md>
        The issue here is that the test <m>f_y=g_x</m> was applied
        improperly. The domain of those functions is not simply connected.
        They are not defined at <m>O(0,0)</m>, thus the domain has a hole
        and it is not simply connected. Moreover, this hole is enclosed by
        the curve of the vector line integral.
      </p>

      <p>
        Another way to arrive at that conclusion is to try to find a
        function for which <m>\nabla \varphi =\vec F</m>. At first glance
        that function should be
        <m>\varphi (x,y)= - \arctan \left ( \frac{x}{y} \right)</m>. But
        this function, and by extension its partials, is not defined on the
        <m>x</m>-axis (<m>y=0</m>). Hence <m>\nabla \varphi</m> is not equal
        to <m>\vec F</m>, since the latter is defined at all points of the
        <m>x</m>-axis apart from the origin <m>O(0,0)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
